y  there was indeed started out with  500,000 markers then it went to a  million markers and the problem why you  needed large sample sizes is because if  you accept the p-value of 0.05 as  statistically significant but since you  are taking 1 million looks you had to  divide that 1 million into 0.05 which  meant you had to have a p-value of 5  times 10 to the minus 8 you had to have  8 zeros in front of it and that's why I  require the large sample size we were  fortunate in 2007 here at the Heart  Institute and simultaneously the group  at Iceland we located the first gene or  genetic variant for coronary artery  disease which is on chromosome 9 on the  small arm which is now known as 9 p21  that was published in science 2007 at  the same time the Icelandic group also  in the same issue of science at the same  region and agreed with our story from  that point of view so 9 p21 was the  first genetic risk factor for coronary  artery disease and heart attacks but  there was a significant surprise in  store for us 9 p21 is very common we  expected that it occurs in 75 percent of  the population it is a risk allele that  today is estimated to occur in about 4.5  billion people it is not a risk factor  from what we can see in African or  African Americans but the rest of the  world Chinese Korean Japanese all of the  East Asians South Asians  Oh M 9 P 21 with a similar frequency and  if they have two copies is about a forty  to fifty percent increased risk for  heart disease if you have one copy about  20 or 25% the real certain surprise of  that study was that 9 p21 locus is  independent of known risk factors for  coronary disease namely it doesn't act  through cholesterol or blood pressure or  diabetes it is acting through as of this  time some unknown mechanism whereby it  mediates increased risk for coronary  disease that was certainly an  interesting and significant finding  because it told us other things are  going on to cause heart disease it isn't  just cholesterol or blood pressure  recognising that we needed large sample  sizes and in 1921 we add genotyped a  total of 23 thousand individuals here in  Ottawa in Texas in Denmark and we  recognized that these variants and only  a small amount of risk associated with  them and in order to discover all of  them or most of them it would require an  even larger sample size and from that we  put together a large international  consortium now called cardiogram which  involved both Oxford Cambridge Leicester  and initially in Germany with also  included University of Pennsylvania  Harvard University University of Texas  along with Stanford and ourselves and  since that others have added this is  certainly one of the largest  undertakings in the history of  Cardiology and we leveraged our  resources probably up to the level of  about two hundred and fifty million  dollars all of us  pursuing genes related to coronary  artery disease  today our initial discovery sample size  is now a hundred and ninety four  thousand cases and controllers which  gives us sensitivity to detect most we  think common variance if they occur in  the population of five percent or  greater and so from that we identified  many new genetic risk variants that have  since been published mainly in Nature  Genetics today to summarize there are  now 50 genetic variants for C ad that  are optional wide significance meaning  that their original discoveries  associated with a p-value of 5 times 10  to the minus 8 and they have all been  replicated in independent populations we  feel the chances that one of these  variants not being a genetic risk is  probably less than one in two million so  with 50 genetic risk variants I'm not  going to go through this table but what  you look at here is 15 of them are  acting through no mechanisms you can see  here several acting through cholesterol  or HDL or triglycerides and that four  are acting to hypertension and one is  acting through formation of thrombosis  and time to 