 of base pairs  away from the snip is that  a DNA region that predisposes to disease  one interesting feature that we did find  if you look at the table of 50 genes for  coronary disease only one of them  directly relates to or is associated  with myocardial infarction per se it's  been recognized by epidemiologist  probably for three or four decades that  if you're a blood group A or B you have  an increased chance of having a heart  attack and that what we found in this  study that in fact blood group A or B or  a B increase the risk for heart attack  by about 20 percent  we found that blood group O is not  associated with any increased risk for  coronary artery disease or myocardial  infarction furthermore the rationale has  become clear the locus for the [ __ ] blood  groups they they encode for proteins  called transferases  these transferase transfer a  carbohydrate moiety to von Willebrand's  factor and that prolongs the half-life  of von willebrand factor von  Willebrand's factor is crucial in the  plotting and formation of flåm BOCES so  with a longer half-life they're more  vulnerable to thrombosis they're more  vulnerable to form a clot in the  coronary artery which precipitates a  heart attack on the other end the o gene  it encodes for a transfer AIDS protein  but probably about a hundred thousand  years ago it was mutated and the protein  it makes is no longer functional so no  carbohydrate moiety is transferred and  von Willebrand's factor retains its  normal lifespan and so there's no  increased risk of a heart attack if you  have blood group O  we've all it's also being shown by  others now that the plasma levels of von  Willebrand's factor our 25% higher in  individuals with blood group a or b as  opposed to old and other studies have  shown that if overall if you have blood  group B you have 11 percent increase if  you have both a and B 20 percent so I  think it clearly indicates the mechanism  for this this does have therapeutic  implications to 