cks is unfolding  what are the advantages of genetic  variants over that of measuring your  cholesterol or what have you  well clearly it doesn't vary with time  it doesn't vary with meals drugs or  gender it can be determined by a single  blood tests and it means that you only  need to get one blood sample per  lifetime since one's DNA does not change  over one's lifetime  now the two things that ultimately will  be we hope applied using this new  information of genetic variants one  would be risk stratification picking up  the disease early for early prevention I  won't say much about that the other is  targets to develop novel therapy at the  present time you look at 9 P 21 it is a  potent risk factor it's just as potent  as being a current smoker or increasing  your LDL by 33 milligrams per deciliter  or increasing your blood pressure by 10  millimeters at the present time these  ages are not incorporated in a routine  practice of cardiology but clearly  they're for that purpose ultimately as  we learn more about them will be  utilized as part of the management but  at the moment it is still on a research  basis part of the reason we're all  concerned and eager and enthusiastic and  excited is because such genetic  information will be much more available  in the very near future it costs  probably half a billion dollars to  sequence the complete human genome of  craig Venter when is was done in 2007  because of changes today sequencing the  rate of sequencing has increased more  than ten thousandfold  the cost of sequencing DNA has decreased  1 million fold this means that in the  very near future while only about 10,000  individuals at the moment have had their  whole genome sequence we think it's a  conservative estimate that millions will  have their whole genome sequence by 2017  and certainly in the 20s it could very  well be a routine the implication being  all of these genetic risk variants that  I talked about for coronary disease of  course and other di